{% assign sc = site.data.policy_feasibility_scenarios %}
{% assign ref = sc.reference %}
{% assign c_t0 = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T0' | first %}
{% assign c_t1 = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T1' | first %}
{% assign c_t1b = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T1b' | first %}
{% assign c_t2 = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T2' | first %}
<section id="pf-model" class="pf-section pf-reveal">
  <p class="pf-step-tag">Scenario use</p>
  <h2><span class="pf-section-number">05</span> Bring the assessment into the scenario</h2>

  <p class="pf-prose">{{ pf.model_application.setting }}</p>
  <div class="pf-settings">
    <div class="pf-setting">
      <p class="pf-setting-label">Adopted policy</p>
      <ul>
        {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
          {% if t.scenario_use == 'Adopted-policy baseline' or t.scenario_use == 'Sensitivity case' %}
            <li data-trajectory="{{ t.id }}">
              <span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>{{ t.scenario_use | remove: 'Adopted-policy ' | capitalize }}</strong>
              {{ t.id }}
              {{ t.short_title }}
            </li>
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="pf-setting">
      <p class="pf-setting-label">Exploratory</p>
      <ul>
        {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
          {% if t.scenario_use == 'Alternative policy future' %}
            <li data-trajectory="{{ t.id }}">
              <span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>Alternative</strong> {{ t.id }}
              {{ t.short_title }}
            </li>
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="pf-quantify">
    <h3>Model runs for {{ sc.model.result_year }}</h3>
    <p class="pf-prose">
      To explore what the choice of trajectory can mean in a model, I ran all four trajectories in a power system model of the Nordic countries for
      {{ sc.model.result_year }}, together with a reference run without the new datacenters. A requirement that counts any new fossil-free generation
      (T1) changes little compared with no requirement (T0), because it is mostly met with onshore wind that the model builds anyway. A requirement
      for additional generation (T1b) and a stop for new datacenters (T2) both give clearly lower prices, especially in the most expensive hours.
    </p>
    {% assign smax = 250.0 %}
    {% assign cmax = 5.0 %}
    <figure class="pf-bars pf-price" aria-labelledby="pf-bars-caption">
      <figcaption id="pf-bars-caption" class="pf-sr-only">
        Electricity price in SE3 and extra cost for other Swedish customers, {{ sc.model.result_year }}
      </figcaption>
      <p class="pf-mix-legend">
        <span><span class="pf-key pf-price-avg" aria-hidden="true"></span>Average spot price</span>
        <span><span class="pf-key pf-price-p99" aria-hidden="true"></span>Most expensive 1% of hours</span>
      </p>
      <div class="pf-price-row pf-price-head" aria-hidden="true">
        <span></span><span>Spot price, SE3 · <span class="pf-nowrap">EUR/MWh</span></span
        ><span></span><span>Extra cost, other customers · <span class="pf-nowrap">bn EUR/yr</span></span
        ><span></span>
      </div>
      {% assign rows = sc.cases | unshift: ref %}
      {% for c in rows %}
        {% assign t = pf.trajectories | where: 'id', c.id | first %}
        <div class="pf-price-row{% if c.id == 'none' %} pf-bar-reference{% endif %}" data-trajectory="{{ c.id }}">
          <span class="pf-bar-label">
            {%- if t -%}
              <small>{{ c.id }}</small><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ t.short_title }}</span>
            {%- else -%}
              <span class="pf-mix-short">Ref.</span><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ ref.label }}</span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
          <span class="pf-price-track">
            <span class="pf-price-p99" style="width: {{ c.values.p99_se3 | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax }}%"></span>
            <span class="pf-price-avg" style="width: {{ c.values.price_se3 | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax }}%"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="pf-bar-value">{{ c.values.price_se3 | round }} · {{ c.values.p99_se3 }}</span>
          <span class="pf-price-track">
            <span class="pf-price-avg" style="width: {{ c.values.other_bill | times: 100.0 | divided_by: cmax }}%"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="pf-bar-value">
            {%- if c.id == 'none' %}–{% else %}{{ c.values.other_bill }}{% endif -%}
          </span>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
      <p class="pf-caption">
        Modelled spot price in bidding zone SE3: the yearly average and the price exceeded in only 1% of the hours. Extra cost for other customers:
        their electricity use multiplied by the hourly wholesale price, summed over the year and compared with the reference run. Most other
        electricity use is fixed in the model, so it does not fall when prices rise. Only part of hydrogen production and some flexible use respond to
        prices. Network tariffs and taxes are not included.
      </p>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div id="pf-additionality" class="pf-callout">
    <h3>Is new generation additional?</h3>
    <p>
      In all runs, the model builds onshore wind in Sweden up to, or very close to, its limit, which corresponds to 70 TWh a year in an average year.
      It does so mainly to meet growing electricity demand. In the Swedish Energy Agency’s current-policy scenario, which the model uses for demand,
      Swedish electricity use grows by about 60 TWh between 2025 and 2035, and about 46 TWh of that comes from industry. In the other runs, the
      announced datacenters add about 36 TWh. Under T1, most of the requirement can therefore be met with onshore wind that the model builds anyway.
      In T0 and T1, most of the added electricity use is covered by imports, although T1 adds some offshore wind. T1b counts only generation that the
      model would not otherwise build, and in that run the model adds new nuclear power and offshore wind instead. The results thus depend on how a
      requirement is specified, not only on whether there is one.
    </p>
    {% assign dm = sc.mix.delta %}
    <figure class="pf-mix" aria-labelledby="pf-mix-caption">
      <figcaption id="pf-mix-caption">{{ dm.title }}</figcaption>
      <p class="pf-mix-legend">
        {% for k in dm.carriers %}
          <span><span class="pf-key pf-mix-{{ k.id }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ k.label }}</span>
        {% endfor %}
      </p>
      {% for r in dm.runs %}
        {% assign t = pf.trajectories | where: 'id', r.id | first %}
        <div class="pf-bar-row pf-mix-row" data-trajectory="{{ r.id }}">
          <span class="pf-bar-label"
            ><small>{{ r.id }}</small><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ t.short_title }}</span></span
          >
          <span class="pf-mix-track">
            {%- for g in r.segments -%}
              {%- assign c = dm.carriers | where: 'id', g.key | first -%}
              <span
                class="pf-mix-part pf-mix-{{ g.key }}{% if g.value < 4 and g.value > -4 %} pf-mix-small{% endif %}"
                style="left: {{ g.left }}%; width: {{ g.width }}%"
                title="{{ c.label }}: {{ g.value }} TWh"
              >
                {%- if g.value >= 4 %}+{{ g.value | round }}{% endif -%}
              </span>
            {%- endfor -%}
            <span class="pf-mix-axis" style="left: {{ dm.axis_left }}%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="pf-bar-value pf-mix-trade">+{{ r.total }} TWh</span>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
      <p class="pf-caption">{{ dm.note }}</p>
    </figure>
    <p class="pf-caption">{{ ad.note }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pf-drawers">
    {% assign lp = sc.load_profiles %}
    <details class="pf-drawer" id="pf-load-profiles">
      <summary><strong>Electricity use in the model</strong><span>Where demand comes from and how it varies over the day and year</span></summary>
      <div class="pf-drawer-body">
        <p class="pf-prose">
          Electricity use per bidding zone follows the Swedish Energy Agency’s current-policy scenario, broken down by county and sector in
          <a href="https://www.behovskartan.se/">Behovskartan</a>, a tool for exploring Sweden’s future electricity needs developed by AI Sweden. The
          scenario’s own growth in datacenter use after 2025 is removed. It is replaced by the projects in the high scenario of the
          <a href="{{ '/projects/datacenter-tracker/' | relative_url }}">Nordic datacenter tracker</a>, a separate project on this site that lists
          announced datacenters and their expected electricity use. Growth after 2025 gets an hourly profile per sector. Electric cars, trucks and
          rail use typical profiles from Behovskartan. Households and services use measured 2023 data, and electric heating follows heat pumps at 2023
          temperatures. Industry and hydrogen production are flat. Large datacenters use the hourly shape of Norwegian datacenters, with monthly
          factors from the datacenter data in Behovskartan. Datacenters for AI and high-performance computing are flat. There are no open hourly
          series per sector for Sweden, so households, services and datacenters use Norwegian measured data from
          <a href="https://data.elhub.no/">Elhub</a>, the Norwegian data hub for electricity metering. The charts show bidding zone SE3, central
          Sweden including Stockholm.
        </p>
        <figure class="pf-loadprof" aria-labelledby="pf-loadprof-caption">
          <p class="pf-mix-legend">
            {% for sr in lp.series %}
              <span><span class="pf-key pf-lp-{{ sr.id }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ sr.label }}</span>
            {% endfor %}
          </p>
          <div class="pf-loadprof-panels">
            <div>
              <p class="pf-loadprof-title">A week in January, {{ lp.zone }}</p>
              <svg viewBox="-28 -8 640 232" role="img" aria-label="Hourly load profiles for one week in January">
                {% for t in lp.week_ticks %}
                  <line class="pf-lp-grid{% if t.v == 1 %} pf-lp-one{% endif %}" x1="0" x2="600" y1="{{ t.y }}" y2="{{ t.y }}" />
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="-8" y="{{ t.y | plus: 4 }}" text-anchor="end">{{ t.v }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for dname in lp.days %}
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="{{ forloop.index0 | times: 600.0 | divided_by: 7 | plus: 42.9 }}" y="222" text-anchor="middle">{{ dname }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for sr in lp.series %}<path class="pf-lp-line pf-lp-{{ sr.id }}" d="{{ sr.week }}" />{% endfor %}
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="pf-loadprof-title">Monthly average, {{ lp.zone }}</p>
              <svg viewBox="-28 -8 340 232" role="img" aria-label="Monthly average of the load profiles">
                {% for t in lp.month_ticks %}
                  <line class="pf-lp-grid{% if t.v == 1 %} pf-lp-one{% endif %}" x1="0" x2="300" y1="{{ t.y }}" y2="{{ t.y }}" />
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="-8" y="{{ t.y | plus: 4 }}" text-anchor="end">{{ t.v }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for mname in lp.months %}
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="{{ forloop.index0 | times: 300.0 | divided_by: 11 }}" y="222" text-anchor="middle">{{ mname }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for sr in lp.series %}<path class="pf-lp-line pf-lp-{{ sr.id }}" d="{{ sr.month }}" />{% endfor %}
              </svg>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="pf-caption" id="pf-loadprof-caption">
            Hourly profiles relative to the yearly average (1 = average). Datacenters use almost the same power every hour. Their peak is about 5%
            above the average, and July is about 7% above January because of cooling.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <div class="pf-closing">
    <div class="pf-model-feedback">
      <p class="pf-caption">Back to the policy assumptions</p>
      {% include policy-feasibility/feedback-loop.liquid %}
      <p>{{ pf.model_application.feedback }}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pf-assumptions">
      <p class="pf-caption">Model and assumptions</p>
      <p>The model is {{ sc.model.name }}. {{ sc.model.setup }} The model is currently in development and results are work in progress.</p>
      <dl>
        {% for a in sc.assumptions %}
          <div>
            <dt>{{ a.label }}</dt>
            <dd>{{ a.value }}</dd>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
